WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.790
In 1993,

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:02.698
investors told him

00:00:02.718 --> 00:00:04.530
video games weren't a market.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.631
His mother asked why

00:00:05.651 --> 00:00:07.240
he didn't just get a job.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.680
Sixteen years later, at Stanford,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.795
Jensen Huang explained

00:00:10.815 --> 00:00:12.230
how he got past that.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:13.310
Lesson one:

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.200
don't wait for a grand vision.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.870
Everyone has a perspective.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.490
That's all a vision really is.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.086
His perspective in 1993:

00:00:20.106 --> 00:00:20.990
take 3D graphics

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.516
off expensive workstations,

00:00:22.536 --> 00:00:23.680
make them cheap,

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.277
and let video games pull

00:00:25.297 --> 00:00:27.510
the whole PC market forward.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:28.830
Lesson two:

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:29.540
sometimes you go

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.930
against your customers.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.676
Dell, HP, IBM and Gateway

00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:34.720
all said it cost too much.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.992
He still bet the company's

00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:37.263
last few million on a chip

00:00:37.283 --> 00:00:38.630
that was far too big,

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.528
sure the technology

00:00:39.548 --> 00:00:40.890
would get cheap fast.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:42.473
About two hundred companies

00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:43.900
went after 3D graphics.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.342
Money, talent and technology

00:00:45.362 --> 00:00:46.640
were roughly equal.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.940
What decided who survived,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.240
he said, was understanding

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.810
why your business works.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.000
Lesson three:

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.800
get comfortable with ambiguity.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.590
Leaders don't have to be outgoing.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:56.516
They do have to act

00:00:56.536 --> 00:00:58.250
before the outcome is clear.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.300
Lesson four:

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.100
tear it down and rebuild.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.920
Every success has to be rebuilt,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.300
roughly once a decade.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.211
If you don't replace

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:05.952
your own product,

00:01:05.972 --> 00:01:07.280
your competitors will.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:07.718
Finally:

00:01:07.738 --> 00:01:09.699
skill, intellect and training matter,

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:11.300
but they're not enough.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.019
You need passion

00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:12.923
for the painful parts

00:01:12.943 --> 00:01:14.200
of building a company.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.880
Which lesson do you need most?

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.140
Tell me in the comments.
